Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:

$$
\begin{aligned}
4 x - 6+\left(2 y + 2\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\
\left(2 y + 2\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\left(4 x - 6\right) \\
\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\frac{4 x - 6}{2 y + 2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope at the given point:

$$
m_{\mathrm{t}}=-\frac{6}{10}=- \frac{3}{5}
$$

The tangent line uses $m=- \frac{3}{5}$.

Use the given point and this slope:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-\left(4\right)&=- \frac{3}{5}\left(x-\left(3\right)\right) \\
y&=- \frac{3}{5}\left(x-\left(3\right)\right)+\left(4\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$. Substitute and simplify:

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=- \frac{3}{5}\left(x-\left(3\right)\right)+\left(4\right) \\
x-\left(3\right)&=-\frac{4}{- \frac{3}{5}} \\
x&=\frac{29}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore $P=\left(\frac{29}{3},0\right)$.

Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. The product rule gives $\frac{d}{dx}(xy)=y+x\frac{dy}{dx}$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y + 2+\left(x - 1\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\
\left(x - 1\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\left(y + 2\right) \\
\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\frac{y + 2}{x - 1}
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope at the given point:

$$
m_{\mathrm{t}}=-\frac{1}{3}=- \frac{1}{3}
$$

The tangent line uses $m=- \frac{1}{3}$.

Use the given point and this slope:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-\left(-1\right)&=- \frac{1}{3}\left(x-\left(4\right)\right) \\
y&=- \frac{1}{3}\left(x-\left(4\right)\right)+\left(-1\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$. Substitute and simplify:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=- \frac{1}{3}\left(0-\left(4\right)\right)+\left(-1\right) \\
y&=\frac{1}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore $P=\left(0,\frac{1}{3}\right)$.

Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:

$$
\begin{aligned}
8 x+\left(6 y\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\
\left(6 y\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\left(8 x\right) \\
\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\frac{8 x}{6 y}
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope at the given point:

$$
m_{\mathrm{t}}=-\frac{16}{-12}=\frac{4}{3}
$$

The tangent line uses $m=\frac{4}{3}$.

Use the given point and this slope:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-\left(-2\right)&=\frac{4}{3}\left(x-\left(2\right)\right) \\
y&=\frac{4}{3}\left(x-\left(2\right)\right)+\left(-2\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$. Substitute and simplify:

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=\frac{4}{3}\left(x-\left(2\right)\right)+\left(-2\right) \\
x-\left(2\right)&=-\frac{-2}{\frac{4}{3}} \\
x&=\frac{7}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore $P=\left(\frac{7}{2},0\right)$.

Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. Differentiate implicitly and collect the derivative terms:

$$
\begin{aligned}
4 x - 2+\left(4 y - 4\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=0 \\
\left(4 y - 4\right)\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\left(4 x - 2\right) \\
\frac{dy}{dx}&=-\frac{4 x - 2}{4 y - 4}
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope at the given point:

$$
m_{\mathrm{t}}=-\frac{14}{-12}=\frac{7}{6}
$$

The tangent line uses $m=\frac{7}{6}$.

Use the given point and this slope:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-\left(-2\right)&=\frac{7}{6}\left(x-\left(4\right)\right) \\
y&=\frac{7}{6}\left(x-\left(4\right)\right)+\left(-2\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$. Substitute and simplify:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=\frac{7}{6}\left(0-\left(4\right)\right)+\left(-2\right) \\
y&=- \frac{20}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore $P=\left(0,- \frac{20}{3}\right)$.
